A||||5||ezsurf||Rear Swaybar||||1||
Z||000000||ezsurf||11-25-2000||06:52 PM||||I finally managed to get the rear swaybar installed on my 86SE.  However, I would like to mention that I had to remove the front lower A-arm bolt and move the lower A-arm aside to feed one of the bushing mounting bracket bolts through the cradle.  The hole lined up right underneath the edge of the A-arm frame bushing.  There was no mention of this in the instructions or in any of the installation guides I have read.  This was an unexpected and unpleasant surprise.  I find it hard to believe I am the first to experience this problem.  In any case, I am mentioning this for the benefit of all future installs.  When the weather clears up, I'll find out how well it handles.  Good luck with your install. - ez.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Will||11-25-2000||11:16 PM||||No, you're not the first. I ran into this as well.<BR>I took two strips of steel (1/4 x 1) longer than the control arm pivot bolt, drilled through holes at the ends of each and bolted them to the bottom of the cradle. The bolts I used to secure the strips were thus separated far enough to clear the control arm pivot. I used nuts inside the cradle. Do not tap the cradle! It is not thick enough to support proper threading.<BR>     I then removed the strips, drilled and tapped holes in them to which I bolted the sway bar brackets. Then I bolted the whole assembly back under the car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||AL||11-26-2000||03:11 AM||||i didn't have to do that at all i just put the bar on and marked where i was going to drill and it went on perfectly.<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||the need for speed||11-26-2000||03:49 AM||||i am not following you removed the front lower a arm bolt and removed the lower a arm?are you talking about the front of the car or the rear?||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||11-26-2000||09:32 AM||||My yellow 86 had a rear swaybar from the Fiero Store, installed by the previous owner. I removed and re-installed it when I did the 3.8 conversion, and there was plenty of clearance between the mounting bolts and the control arms. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ezsurf||11-26-2000||12:32 PM||||I am refering to the rear of the car.  Remember, this is a rear swaybar. On the lower rear A-arm, there is a front and rear mounting bolt that attaches the A-arm mounting bushings the the cradle. I had to remove the front bolt and pull the A-arm out about 1/2 inch so I could get the swaybar mounting bracket bolt through the frame underneath the A-arm bushing.  Once I got the bolt through the hole, I have about 1 to 2 mm clearance between the top of the bolt and the bottom of the A-arm cradle bushing.  As I mentioned, I hadn't heard of any one encountering such a problem, so I was a bit surprised.  I am using the 7/8 inch Addco swaybar. Since this is the only one I am aware of for the 84-87 models, I find it odd that no one else has mentioned it. Hope this helps clarify the matter.||||reg||1
